Excellent observation! You're absolutely right - the "stop delay" parameter in ESXi autostart is misleading.

What Stop Delay ACTUALLY Does
Stop Delay = How long ESXi waits after issuing shutdown command before moving to the next VM in the shutdown sequence.

ESXi issues: vmware-tools shutdown to VM1
   ↓
Waits: Stop Delay seconds
   ↓  
ESXi issues: vmware-tools shutdown to VM2
   ↓
(and so on...)
The Problem You Noticed
VMs don't actually wait for each other to finish shutting down!

Example with 120-second stop delay:

00:00 - ESXi sends shutdown to DC
00:00 - ESXi waits 120 seconds
02:00 - ESXi sends shutdown to vCenter (DC still shutting down!)
02:00 - ESXi waits 120 seconds
04:00 - ESXi sends shutdown to next VM

All VMs are shutting down in parallel!
When Stop Delay Actually Matters
Only useful if:

VM graceful shutdown is FASTER than the delay - then delay creates actual spacing
You want to stagger shutdown commands - reduce I/O spike by not hitting all VMs simultaneously
Example where it works:

VM takes 30 seconds to shutdown
Stop delay = 60 seconds
Result: 30-second gap between each VM actually powering off
Example where it DOESN'T work (your case):

VM takes 180 seconds to shutdown (like vCenter/SQL)
Stop delay = 120 seconds
Result: Next VM gets shutdown command while previous VM still running!
The Real Solution: Startup Delay is Better
Startup Delay actually works because:

VM1 powers on
   ↓
ESXi waits: Startup Delay
   ↓
VM1 is booting (has the time it needs)
   ↓
VM2 powers on
This creates actual spacing because VMs start sequentially, not parallel.

