You're absolutely correct - I was wrong!

How ESXi Stop Delay ACTUALLY Works
ESXi waits for WHICHEVER comes first:

VM completes shutdown, OR
Stop delay timer expires
VM1: Shutdown command sent
   ↓
VM1: Finishes in 30 seconds ✓
   ↓
IMMEDIATELY moves to VM2 (doesn't wait full 120 seconds)
vs.

VM1: Shutdown command sent
   ↓
VM1: Still shutting down after 120 seconds...
   ↓
Stop delay expires → ESXi moves to VM2 anyway (VM1 still running!)
So Stop Delay is Actually a Timeout, Not a Wait
Stop Delay = Maximum time ESXi will wait for graceful shutdown before moving on

Benefits:

✅ Fast-shutting VMs don't delay the sequence unnecessarily
✅ Prevents one stuck VM from blocking entire shutdown
✅ Acts as safety timeout for VMs without VMware Tools
Problem:

❌ If VM needs 180 seconds but delay is 120 seconds → ESXi moves on before VM finishes
❌ No actual dependency enforcement (next VM can start shutting down while previous still running)
Correct Understanding
Stop Delay should be set to:

Longer than your slowest VM's shutdown time
Example: vCenter takes 3 minutes → set stop delay to 240 seconds
This ensures ESXi waits for actual shutdown completion rather than timing out prematurely.

